        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-14\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line utility that validates YAML content for syntactic correctness. The validator must be built using only existing project dependencies (Bun built-ins and TypeScript standard libraries) with absolutely no new external packages added to the project. Users invoke the command to check whether a given YAML file or string is well-formed and receive clear pass/fail feedback with error details on failure.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers of this utility project currently have no way to validate YAML files from the command line within the project toolset. YAML is a common configuration format and syntax errors in YAML files can cause downstream failures that are difficult to diagnose. A lightweight, dependency-free validator would fill this gap.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Provides a practical, reusable utility consistent with the project's purpose as a utility scripting toolkit.\n- Maintains the project's zero-external-dependency philosophy, keeping the project lightweight and auditable.\n- Offers immediate feedback on YAML syntax issues before files are consumed by other tools.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n- SM-1: The validator correctly identifies valid YAML and reports it as valid.\n- SM-2: The validator correctly identifies invalid YAML and reports meaningful error information (line number, description of the problem).\n- SM-3: No new entries appear in `package.json` `dependencies` or `devDependencies` (beyond the existing `@types/bun`).\n- SM-4: The command runs successfully via `bun run` with no additional install steps.\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| Developer | A developer using this project's CLI utilities | Validate YAML files quickly from the command line without installing additional tools |\n| CI Pipeline | Automated build/test processes | Programmatic YAML validation with clear exit codes for pass/fail |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainer | Keep the project dependency-free, consistent patterns, test coverage |\n| End User (Developer) | Reliable, fast YAML validation with clear error messages |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Validating YAML syntax (well-formedness) for a given file path or standard input.\n- Reporting success or failure with a human-readable message.\n- Reporting error location (line number) and error description when validation fails.\n- Returning appropriate exit codes (0 for valid, non-zero for invalid or error).\n- Supporting single-document and multi-document YAML files (documents separated by `---`).\n- Supporting common YAML data types: strings, numbers, booleans, null, sequences (lists), and mappings (objects).\n- Recognizing and correctly handling YAML comments (lines starting with `#`).\n- Detecting common YAML syntax errors: incorrect indentation, invalid characters, unclosed quotes, malformed key-value pairs, duplicate keys at the same level, and tab characters used for indentation.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating YAML content against a JSON Schema, OpenAPI spec, or custom schema).\n- YAML transformation, formatting, or pretty-printing.\n- YAML-to-JSON or JSON-to-YAML conversion.\n- Editing, fixing, or auto-correcting invalid YAML.\n- Support for YAML tags, anchors, aliases, or advanced YAML features beyond basic structural validation.\n- Network-based validation (fetching YAML from URLs).\n- GUI or web-based interface.\n- Watch mode or file-system monitoring.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- A-1: The project runs on Bun and can leverage Bun's built-in APIs and TypeScript standard library.\n- A-2: YAML parsing logic will be implemented from scratch or use only built-in Bun/Node.js APIs (no `js-yaml`, `yaml`, or similar npm packages).\n- A-3: The validator targets YAML 1.2 basic syntax (structural correctness), not full spec compliance for all edge cases.\n- A-4: Input files are expected to be UTF-8 encoded.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: File Path Input\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator must accept a file path as a command-line argument and validate the YAML content of that file.\n**Rationale**: File-based validation is the primary use case for checking configuration files.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-001a: Running the command with a valid file path reads and validates that file.\n- AC-001b: Running the command with a non-existent file path outputs a clear error message and exits with a non-zero code.\n- AC-001c: Running the command with no arguments outputs usage instructions to stderr and exits with a non-zero code.\n\n### REQ-002: Valid YAML Reporting\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI Pipeline\n**Requirement**: When the input YAML is syntactically valid, the validator must output a success message to stdout and exit with code 0.\n**Rationale**: Clear affirmative feedback confirms the file is well-formed.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-002a: A valid YAML file produces a message containing the word \"valid\" (case-insensitive) on stdout.\n- AC-002b: The process exit code is 0.\n\n### REQ-003: Invalid YAML Error Reporting\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI Pipeline\n**Requirement**: When the input YAML is syntactically invalid, the validator must output an error message to stderr that includes the line number where the error was detected and a human-readable description of the problem, then exit with a non-zero code.\n**Rationale**: Actionable error messages allow the user to locate and fix the problem quickly.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-003a: An invalid YAML file produces an error message on stderr containing a line number.\n- AC-003b: The error message includes a human-readable description of the syntax problem.\n- AC-003c: The process exit code is non-zero (1 for validation failure).\n\n### REQ-004: Zero New Dependencies\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Project Maintainer\n**Requirement**: The implementation must not add any new entries to `package.json` `dependencies` or `devDependencies`. Only Bun built-in APIs and TypeScript standard library constructs are permitted.\n**Rationale**: The project follows a zero-external-dependency philosophy as documented in the project patterns.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-004a: After implementation, `package.json` `dependencies` remains absent or empty.\n- AC-004b: After implementation, `package.json` `devDependencies` contains only `@types/bun` (the pre-existing entry).\n- AC-004c: No `node_modules` additions beyond what Bun provides natively.\n\n### REQ-005: Exit Code Semantics\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: CI Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The command must use well-defined exit codes: 0 for valid YAML, 1 for invalid YAML, and 2 for operational errors (file not found, permission denied, etc.).\n**Rationale**: CI pipelines rely on exit codes for automated pass/fail decisions.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-005a: Exit code 0 when input YAML is valid.\n- AC-005b: Exit code 1 when input YAML has syntax errors.\n- AC-005c: Exit code 2 when the file cannot be read or other operational error occurs.\n\n### REQ-006: Basic YAML Syntax Coverage\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator must correctly parse and validate the following YAML constructs: key-value mappings, nested mappings (via indentation), sequences (list items with `-`), scalar values (strings, numbers, booleans, null), quoted strings (single and double), comments (lines beginning with `#`), and multi-document files (separated by `---`).\n**Rationale**: These constructs represent the vast majority of real-world YAML usage in configuration files.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-006a: A file with nested key-value mappings is accepted as valid.\n- AC-006b: A file with list items using `-` prefix is accepted as valid.\n- AC-006c: A file with single-quoted and double-quoted strings is accepted as valid.\n- AC-006d: A file containing only comments is accepted as valid.\n- AC-006e: A file with `---` document separators is accepted as valid.\n\n### REQ-007: Common Error Detection\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator should detect and report the following common YAML errors: tab characters used for indentation, inconsistent indentation levels, unclosed quoted strings, invalid mapping syntax (missing colon, colon without space), and duplicate keys at the same mapping level.\n**Rationale**: These are the most frequent YAML authoring mistakes, and detecting them provides the most value.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-007a: A file using tabs for indentation is reported as invalid with a message mentioning tabs.\n- AC-007b: A file with inconsistent indentation is reported as invalid.\n- AC-007c: A file with an unclosed quoted string is reported as invalid.\n- AC-007d: A file with duplicate keys at the same level is reported as invalid or warned.\n\n### REQ-008: Command Integration\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator command should be runnable via a script entry in `package.json` (e.g., `bun run validate-yaml`) in addition to direct invocation (`bun run src/yaml-validator.ts`).\n**Rationale**: Consistent with existing project patterns where commands are registered as npm scripts.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-008a: A `validate-yaml` (or similarly named) script entry exists in `package.json` `scripts`.\n- AC-008b: Running the script with a file argument works identically to direct invocation.\n\n### REQ-009: Standard Input Support\n**Priority**: Could Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator could accept YAML content piped via standard input (stdin) when no file argument is provided or when a `-` argument is given.\n**Rationale**: Stdin support enables integration with pipes and other command-line tools.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC-009a: Piping YAML content to the command with `-` as the argument validates the piped content.\n- AC-009b: The same success/failure output and exit code conventions apply to stdin input.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n- NFR-001: The validator should complete validation of a typical configuration file (under 1000 lines) in under 1 second.\n- NFR-002: The validator should handle files up to 10,000 lines without crashing or excessive memory usage.\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- NFR-003: The validator must only read files specified by the user. It must not write to or modify any files.\n- NFR-004: The validator must not execute or interpret any code embedded in YAML content.\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- NFR-005: Error messages must be understandable by a developer without referencing the YAML specification. Include the file name, line number, and a plain-English description.\n- NFR-006: The command must display usage help when invoked with no arguments or with `--help`.\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- NFR-007: The validator must target YAML 1.2 basic syntax conventions. Full YAML 1.2 spec compliance (anchors, aliases, tags, custom types) is not required.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a Configuration File\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to run a command that checks my YAML configuration file for syntax errors\n**So that** I catch formatting mistakes before they cause runtime failures\n\n**Acceptance**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file at a known path\n- WHEN I run the validator command with that file path\n- THEN I see either a success message (if valid) or an error message with line number and description (if invalid)\n\n### STORY-002: CI Pipeline YAML Gate\n**As a** CI pipeline\n**I want** to run the YAML validator as a build step that exits with code 0 on success and non-zero on failure\n**So that** broken YAML configuration files fail the build before deployment\n\n**Acceptance**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file in the repository\n- WHEN the validator runs in a CI environment\n- THEN exit code 0 means the file is valid and the pipeline continues\n- AND exit code 1 means the file is invalid and the pipeline fails\n\n### STORY-003: Helpful Error for Bad Indentation\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to see a clear error message when my YAML has indentation problems\n**So that** I can fix the issue without guessing what went wrong\n\n**Acceptance**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with inconsistent indentation or tabs\n- WHEN I run the validator\n- THEN the error message mentions the specific line and describes the indentation problem\n\n### STORY-004: Check File Before Committing\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to quickly validate a YAML file from the command line\n**So that** I know it is well-formed before I commit it to the repository\n\n**Acceptance**:\n- GIVEN any YAML file I am editing\n- WHEN I run `bun run validate-yaml path/to/file.yaml`\n- THEN I get immediate feedback on whether the file is valid\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n- BR-001: The project must not gain any new external dependencies. This is a hard constraint driven by the project's design philosophy.\n- BR-002: The validator is a read-only tool. It must never modify input files.\n- BR-003: The validator should follow existing project conventions for file naming (kebab-case), function naming (camelCase), and error handling (try/catch with descriptive messages).\n- BR-004: The validator must have accompanying unit tests using Bun's built-in test runner, co-located with the source as `*.test.ts`.\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Type | Impact |\n|----------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime dependency (existing) | Validator must run under Bun |\n| TypeScript strict mode | Development constraint (existing) | Code must compile under strict mode |\n| No new npm packages | Hard constraint (from requirement) | All parsing logic must be hand-written or use Bun/Node built-ins |\n| ESM module system | Development constraint (existing) | Must use `import`/`export` syntax |\n| Existing project structure | Convention constraint | New files follow `src/` directory layout and kebab-case naming |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| What counts as \"simple\" validation? | Syntax/structural correctness only, not schema validation | Inferred from \"simple\" qualifier in requirement |\n| Which YAML version? | YAML 1.2 basic syntax | Conservative default; most common version |\n| Should it support stdin? | Marked as Could Have; file path is primary input | Conservative scoping of \"simple\" requirement |\n| What about YAML anchors/aliases? | Out of scope (advanced features) | Consistent with \"simple\" qualifier |\n| Should duplicate keys be errors or warnings? | Reported as invalid (conservative) | YAML 1.2 spec discourages duplicate keys |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD association | None | No PRDs found in `.rp1/work/prds/` directory |\n| YAML version target | YAML 1.2 basic syntax | Most widely used version; conservative default |\n| Primary input method | File path argument | Most common use case for \"simple\" CLI validator |\n| Error output destination | stderr | Unix convention for error output; enables clean piping |\n| Exit code scheme | 0/1/2 (valid/invalid/error) | Standard Unix convention |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" is vague -- scope unclear | Syntax validation only; no schema, transform, or format features | Inferred from \"simple\" qualifier and zero-dependency constraint |\n| \"command\" form unspecified | Bun-runnable TypeScript file with `package.json` script entry | KB patterns.md: project uses `bun run` scripts |\n| \"no new deps\" boundary unclear | No additions to `dependencies` or `devDependencies`; Bun built-ins are allowed | KB patterns.md: \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\" |\n| User types not specified | Developer (primary) and CI Pipeline (secondary) | Inferred from CLI command context and common YAML validation use cases |\n| Test expectations not stated | Unit tests required using Bun test runner, co-located as `*.test.ts` | KB patterns.md: established testing convention |\n| Advanced YAML features (anchors, tags) | Excluded from scope | \"Simple\" qualifier plus zero-dependency constraint makes full spec impractical |\n"
      },
