        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-14\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Parser approach | Line-by-line state tracker | Validation-only use case does not need an AST; line-by-line processing maps naturally to line-number error reporting; simpler and less error-prone than recursive descent for the scoped YAML 1.2 subset | Recursive descent parser (more complex, produces AST we don't need); Regex-only approach (too fragile for nested structures and multi-line constructs) |\n| D2 | Module decomposition | 3 modules: types, validator core, CLI entry | Clean separation of concerns -- types are shared, validator contains testable pure logic, CLI handles I/O side effects and process lifecycle | Single monolithic file (too large, harder to unit test pure logic); More granular split with separate tokenizer/state-tracker files (over-engineering given ~500 LOC scope) |\n| D3 | Error reporting strategy | Accumulate all errors, report at end | Users benefit from seeing all problems at once rather than fixing one error, re-running, finding the next; aligns with linter UX conventions | Fail-fast on first error (simpler implementation but significantly worse developer experience) |\n| D4 | Indentation tracking algorithm | Stack-based indent tracker | Natural fit for YAML's hierarchical nesting model; efficient O(depth) space; clean push/pop semantics for entering/exiting nesting levels | Simple counter (cannot detect inconsistent indentation within siblings); Regex-only validation (cannot track nesting context across lines) |\n| D5 | Duplicate key detection scope | Per-indentation-level Map with reset on dedent | Matches YAML 1.2 spec semantics where duplicate keys are prohibited at the same mapping level but the same key name at different nesting depths is valid | Global key tracking (incorrect semantics -- same key at different levels is valid YAML); Skip duplicate detection entirely (fails REQ-007d) |\n| D6 | CLI argument parsing | Positional argument via process.argv[2] | Matches existing project pattern (src/index.ts uses process.argv[2]); simple, conventional, no library needed | Flag-based parsing with --file/--input options (over-engineered for single-purpose command; would need argument parsing logic or a library) |\n| D7 | Exit code scheme | 0 = valid, 1 = invalid YAML, 2 = operational error | Standard Unix convention explicitly required by REQ-005; three-tier scheme allows CI pipelines to distinguish between \"bad YAML\" and \"could not read file\" | Two-tier 0/1 only (loses the operational-error distinction that CI pipelines need for proper error handling) |\n| D8 | Stdin support inclusion | Include via \"-\" argument | Low additional implementation complexity (~10 LOC); high utility for CI pipe workflows; Bun.stdin.text() provides clean built-in API | Omit entirely (loses pipe integration; stdin is a Could Have but the cost is minimal) |\n| D9 | Quote handling scope | Single-line and multi-line quoted strings (single and double quotes) | Covers the vast majority of real-world YAML quoted string usage; essential for avoiding false-positive errors on colons/hashes inside strings | Single-line only (would miss valid multi-line quoted strings); Full YAML block scalar support with |/> (significantly more complex, diminishing returns for validation-only use case) |\n| D10 | File I/O API | Bun.file() + .text() | Bun-native API, async, returns UTF-8 string directly; no dependencies; consistent with project's Bun-first approach | Node.js fs.readFileSync (synchronous, less idiomatic for Bun); Node.js fs/promises (works but Bun.file is preferred in Bun projects) |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | KB index.md | Project uses Bun runtime exclusively |\n| Language | TypeScript strict mode | KB patterns.md, tsconfig.json | Project enforces strict TypeScript |\n| Module system | ESM (import/export) | KB patterns.md | Project uses \"type\": \"module\" |\n| File naming | kebab-case | KB patterns.md | Established project convention |\n| Function naming | camelCase | KB patterns.md | Established project convention |\n| Type naming | PascalCase | KB patterns.md | Established project convention |\n| Test framework | bun:test with describe/test/expect | KB patterns.md | Existing test pattern |\n| Test location | Co-located *.test.ts | KB patterns.md | Existing test pattern |\n| File I/O | Bun.file() | KB architecture.md | Bun built-in, zero dependencies |\n| Error handling | try/catch with descriptive messages | KB patterns.md | Existing error handling pattern |\n| YAML parsing library | None (hand-written) | KB patterns.md, REQ-004 | \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\" |\n| Parser architecture | Line-by-line state tracker | Conservative default | Simplest approach sufficient for validation-only; avoids AST overhead |\n| Error accumulation | Collect all errors | Conservative default | Better developer experience than fail-fast |\n"
